==================================================
COMMAND: define_attribute
==================================================
  define_attribute: defines a new attribute 

Usage: define_attribute -category <string> -data_type <string> [-default <string>] [-help_string <string>] [-check_function <string>] [-compute_function <string>] [-set_function <string>]
           [-quiet] [-skip_in_db] -obj_type <string> [ -hidden | -obsolete ] <string>

    -category <string>:
        category name 
    -data_type <string>:
        data type (int, coord, bool, string, double, object, object*, in_file, out_file, in_dir, out_dir, point, rect, polygon) 
    [-default <string>]:
        default value 
    [-help_string <string>]:
        help string 
    [-check_function <string>]:
        name of Tcl proc used to check value. The Tcl proc returns '1' if value is OK, '0' otherwise and requires two arguments: <object> <value> 
    [-compute_function <string>]:
        name of Tcl proc used to compute value. If this option is used the attribute becomes read-only, since its value is always computed. The Tcl proc returns the new value and requires 
        one argument: <object> 
    [-set_function <string>]:
        name of Tcl proc used to set value. The Tcl proc returns the new value and requires three arguments: <object> <new_value> <current_value> 
    [-quiet]:
        suppress error messages 
    [-skip_in_db]:
        if set, command write_db will not store attribute settings in the database 
    -obj_type <string>:
        object type 
    [-hidden]:
        hide attribute 
    [-obsolete]:
        obsolete attribute 
    <string>:
        attribute name 

