==================================================
COMMAND: fix_dft_violations
==================================================
  fix_dft_violations: fixes DFT rule violations 

Usage: fix_dft_violations [-async_set] [-async_reset] [-tristate_net] [-clock] [-xsource] [-exclude_xsources <inst|hinst>+] [-violations <violation>+]+ [-add_observe_scan] [-preview]
           [-dont_check_dft_rules] [-dont_map] [-test_control <test_signal>] [-scan_clock_pin <pin|hpin|port>] [-rise] [-fall] [-async_control <test_signal>] [-design <design>]

    [-async_set]:
        fix asynchronous set violations 
    [-async_reset]:
        fix asynchronous reset violations 
    [-tristate_net]:
        fix tristate net contention violations 
    [-clock]:
        fix clock violations 
    [-xsource]:
        fix x-source violations 
    [-exclude_xsources <inst|hinst>+]:
        excludes instances from auto-fix for x-sources 
    [-violations <violation>+]:
        specifies the violations to be fixed (in the form {vid_0 vid_1 ...}) 
    [-add_observe_scan]:
        insert flops for observability 
    [-preview]:
        show what would be done only, but do not modify the design 
    [-dont_check_dft_rules]:
        do not run check_dft_rules while fixing violations 
    [-dont_map]:
        do not techmap the inserted logic even though design is mapped 
    [-test_control <test_signal>]:
        test signal used to fix the DFT violations 
    [-scan_clock_pin <pin|hpin|port>]:
        use this clock pin to fix clock violations or to trigger observation flops 
    [-rise]:
        use rising edge of test clock to fix clock violations 
    [-fall]:
        use falling edge of test clock to fix clock violations 
    [-async_control <test_signal>]:
        test signal to control the async violations being fixed 
    [-design <design>]:
        top-block (required if there are multiple top designs) 

