==================================================
COMMAND: get_computed_shapes
==================================================
  get_computed_shapes: shape manipulation 

Usage: get_computed_shapes [-output <string>] [-step <string>] [-maxPoint <integer>] [-d] [<string>]

    [-output <string>]:
        output format: {polygon rect hrect area} default is rect 
    [-step <string>]:
        step size if output format is rect or hrect 
    [-maxPoint <integer>]:
        maximum number of points for output in a polygon, minimum 5 and maximum 8000 
    [-d]:
        user specified values and return values are in database units: default is um 
    [<string>]:
        shapeList: a polygon {{x y} {x y} ...}, rect {xl yl xu yu} or list of polygons and/or rects {{{x1 y1} {x2 y2} ...} {xl yl xu yu} ...}; Operator: 
        AND|ANDNOT|OR|XOR|SIZE|MOVE|BBOX|INSIDE|OUTSIDE|ENCLOSE|STRADDLE|HOLES|NOHOLES 

==================================================
COMMAND: get_db
==================================================
  get_db: get objects/attributes 

Usage: get_db [-category <string>] [-dbu] [-foreach <string>] [ -if <string> | -expr <string> ] [-index <string>] [-invert] [-regexp] [-match_hier] [-unique] [-computed] [-depth <string>]
           [<string>] [<string>+]

    [-category <string>]:
        specified root attribute category based on predefined list of categories. Ex. tui 
    [-dbu]:
        indicates that attribute (coord, pt, rect, polygon, area, etc.) will be in database units 
    [-foreach <string>]:
        tcl commands to call once for each element using $obj Tcl array 
    -if <string>:
        a Tcl style expression, with extensions to make pattern matching simpler == does not require a quoted string, =~ allows simple patterns, as well as other standard Tcl expressions 
    -expr <string>:
        a Tcl expr with $obj array for any attribute of each object 
    [-index <string>]:
        list of indices in a form of pairs of index_name value or a object name 
    [-invert]:
        inverts the <pattern> and -if <expression> filter to return the objects/attributes that do not match instead of the ones that do 
    [-regexp]:
        indicates that the simple pattern matching cases in <pattern> and <expression> will use regexp syntax instead of the simple * and ? style 
    [-match_hier]:
        indicates that the * in the <pattern> and <expression> expands to the next hierarchical divider character (/) instead of a complete string match 
    [-unique]:
        indicates that the return list will have duplicate entries removed 
    [-computed]:
        forces get_db to output actual values of attributes marked as 'computed' 
    [-depth <string>]:
        a pair of minimum/maximum logical hierarchy levels for the search 
    [<string>]:
        <start> can be any object list (or collection) or <shorthand> (e.g., nets, insts, etc) 
    [<string>+]:
        the <chain> is in the form [.<attribute_name1>[.<attribute_name2>]...] 

